 true] [17592186064384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398157 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186068928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397793 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186078410 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398854 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186078410 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399056 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186078588 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397849 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186078590 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397890 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186079222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186078080 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186079222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399241 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186079222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186079222 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5x+6y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1).$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186079222 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = 5x + 6y, y(0) = 1$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1)$." 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186079222 1074 "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5x+6y \\\\  & =5\\cdot (0)+6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $1$ | $7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈7.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186083238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398101 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186083261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398227 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186083312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399214 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186083390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398395 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186087707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398339 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186087707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398346 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186087755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398995 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186087755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399002 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186087773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398686 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186087773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398693 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186087773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399207 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186087790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397842 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186087873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397786 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186087888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399179 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186092334 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398833 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186096703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398206 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186096703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399342 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186096769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398136 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186096843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398248 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186096843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398255 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186096843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398262 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095730 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186097306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398896 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097306 1071 "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} =-10$." 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186097306 1071 "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=-10.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097306 1074 "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-10\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-10x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-10\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D \\\\  & =-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095736 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186097307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398902 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097307 1071 "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097307 1071 "Solve the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - e^{3x} = 0$." 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186097307 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\,{e}^{3x})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095760 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186097311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398908 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097311 1071 "If $F^{′}(t) = ⟨e^{t} - 4, \\sin t⟩$, then $F(t) =$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186097311 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$, then $F(t)=$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097311 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t}-4,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int ({e}^{t}-4)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}-4t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186096150 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186097376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399124 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097376 1071 "If $F^{′}(t) = ⟨3t^{2}, e^{t}⟩$, then $F(t) =$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186097376 1071 "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097376 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}+{c}_{1},\\,{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\,{e}^{t}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\,{e}^{t}⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186096204 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186097385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399228 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097385 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $x^{2} = 12y$?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186097385 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=12y?$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186097385 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(3)y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=3.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186100876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398360 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186100876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398939 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186100957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398451 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186100957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398458 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186100957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398465 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186100994 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398269 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186100995 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398276 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186101003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398651 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186105715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399090 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186105715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399097 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186105717 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398868 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186110579 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398164 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186110579 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398946 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186110589 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398444 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186110589 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398974 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186115226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399063 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186115242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398706 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186115248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398988 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186115255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398374 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186115255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398381 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186115262 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398423 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186115362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398582 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186115737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113358 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186115737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398700 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186115737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186115737 1071 "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186115737 1071 "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\left.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\left.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\right|___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186115737 1074 "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186119778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398290 13194139533472 17592186045585 tr