rac{3}{5}\\cos tj + \\frac{4}{5}\\cos tk, t ∈ [0, 2π)$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186120248 1071 "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(0,3,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ) \\\\ T(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186120248 1074 "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,3,4)$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,3,4⟩ \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+3\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components of $i$, $j$, and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=3 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,3,4)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k}{1}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-i)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(i\\cdot j)+\\frac{4}{5}(i\\cdot k) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186120525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119234 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186120525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399009 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186120525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186120525 1071 "Given that\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$,\nhas the LU decomposition $A = LU$, then $l_{21} + u_{22} =$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186120525 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{aligned}],$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186120525 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186120528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186119252 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186120528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399050 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186120528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186120528 1071 "Given the curve $r(t) = ⟨e^{t}, \\sqrt{1 + t^{2}}⟩$, find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨0, 1⟩$." 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186120528 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186120528 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩ & =0 \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{t}+1\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{0}\\,,\\,\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}}⟩=⟨1\\,,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(1,1).$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398367 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398953 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398960 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124505 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398297 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124505 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398304 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124512 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398596 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124522 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398820 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124539 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399221 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124591 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398630 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124591 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399281 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398603 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124606 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399130 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124652 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398589 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399186 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398665 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398672 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398658 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398679 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121181 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186124769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397856 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124769 1071 "Given that $y < 80$ and $t \\ge 0$, which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186124769 1071 "Given that $y<80$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186124769 1074 "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(80-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=80.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<80$ for all $t\\ge 0.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186125195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123740 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186125195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398920 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186125195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186125195 1071 "For the function $f(x, y, z) = xy^{2} - 2yz + z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x, y, z) = (1,-1, 1)$." 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186125195 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-2yz+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186125195 1074 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}-2yz+z)\\,k \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\text{ }i+(2xy-2z)\\text{ }j+(-2y+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+(2(1)(-1)-2(1))\\,j+(-2(-1)+1)\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i-4j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\,i-4\\,j+3\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1).$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186125267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124172 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186125267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399322 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186125267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186125267 1071 "Let $f(x, y, z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $AB^(―)$, where $a = ⟨3, 2, 5⟩$ and $b = ⟨1, 4, 1⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B$, respectively. If $f(a) = 2$ and $f(b) = 3$, then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C ∈ AB^(―)$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\left.\\right|_______________$.*" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186125267 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨3,2,5⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4,1⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=2$ and $f(b)=3,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186125267 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 3-2 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨1,4,1⟩-⟨3,2,5⟩) \\\\ 1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,2,-4⟩ \\\\ 1 & =-2{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-4{f}_{z}(c) \\\\ -1 & =2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c)=-1.$ *" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186129134 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399193 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186129134 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399200 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186129134 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399247 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186129255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398402 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186129255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398409 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186129257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398416 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186133790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398437 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186133894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397956 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186133935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398568 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186133935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398575 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186133961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397807 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186133961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398847 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186133963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398840 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130600 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186134079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397897 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134079 1071 "Let $A$ be a $m \\cdot n$ matrix. What is the image of $R^{n}$ under the action of $A$?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186134079 1071 "Let $A$ be a $m\\times n$ matrix. What is the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $A?$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134079 1074 "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & ⋯ & {a}_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+⋯+{x}_{n}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{n} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $A$ must be $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131533 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186134254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398814 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134254 1071 "Find a basis of $H^{⊥}$ if $H = Span{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}$." 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186134254 1071 "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134254 1074 "Let's denote the vector that spans the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}.$ \nSo, every vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131953 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186134324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398827 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134324 1071 "$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $V=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V,$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186134324 1071 "$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}, B = {[ \\mid; v_{1}; \\mid ], [ \\mid; v_{2}; \\mid ], \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nSuppose that $V = Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$. Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$, and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186134324 1074 "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{10}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{15}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{46}{46}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186147434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399260 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186147434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399267 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186147437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399110 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186147437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399274 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186147511 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398739 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186148143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146582 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186148143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398914 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186148143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186148143 1071 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 144y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8m/s$, find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186148143 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186148143 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=144\\,⟹\\,\\omega =12.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=12R\\operatorname{cos}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=12R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186148213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186147002 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186148213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399254 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186148213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186148213 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t} - e^{4t}$ for $s > 4$." 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186148213 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}$ for $s>4.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186148213 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+L{-{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+2},\\,s>-2 \\\\ L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)-(s+2)}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{(s+2)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{-2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-2,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (4,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-2,\\infty )\\cap (4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398637 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399158 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399165 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397862 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397869 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397876 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397883 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399288 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152241 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398388 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398732 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149815 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186152568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398726 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152568 1071 "![](images/bc/bcf4076849dd96858c586f8696414789b3f68074b87652601245407142f2b8fc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t)$, where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186152568 1071 "![](images/bc/bcf4076849dd96858c586f8696414789b3f68074b87652601245407142f2b8fc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186152568 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we scale $y=u(t)$ by a factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(t).$\n\n- There is no horizontal shift or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(t).$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186157452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398719 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186157452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399144 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186157452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399151 13194139533472 