86162292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397909 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186162292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186162292 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186162292 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $λ_{1} = λ_{2} =-2$, and $λ_{3} = 4$. Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186162292 1074 "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block ${J}_{1}(4)$ corresponding to $\\lambda =4.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =-2:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block ${J}_{2}(-2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely ${J}_{1}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\n\nComputing $A+2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ there will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ say, ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186162309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159026 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186162309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397998 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186162309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186162309 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 8 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-4.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186162309 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-8 