585 true] [17592186198616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194297 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186198616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398534 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186198616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186198616 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨x^{2}, y^{2}, z^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z = x + y$ for $0 \\le x \\le 2$ and $0 \\le y \\le 2$. Using the parametrization $x = u$ and $y = v$, the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}AAAAAAAdvdu$.\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186198616 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\,{y}^{2},\\,{z}^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186198616 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u+v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2},\\,{y}^{2},\\,{z}^{2}⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u+v} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,(u+v)^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,(u+v)^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}+{u}^{2}+2uv+{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\,{y}^{2},\\,{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2uv\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186198728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194964 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186198728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398624 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186198728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186198728 1071 "$\\,$ $x\\in R$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)?$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186198728 1071 "$x ∈ R$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)$?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186198728 1074 "In plain English, our predicate $P(x)$ means the following: \n\n$\\,$ $x$ is a member of the set of real numbers.\n\nThe symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. To negate our plain English expression, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ is a member of the set of real numbers.\n\nIn other words, $x$ is *not* a member of $R.$ Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\notin R$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186221426 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398787 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186221467 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398780 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186222005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220672 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186222005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398794 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186222005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186222005 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186222005 1071 "$x^{′} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x, x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos 2t \\\\ \\sin 2t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin 2t \\\\ \\cos 2t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$, shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 false] [17592186222005 1074 "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397787 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397786 1069 "q-103567" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397786 1071 "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397786 1072 #uuid "420cf089-e2ca-55d0-b4d5-3e91f54aadf7" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397786 1074 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =y\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-x{e}^{2x}$ and $Q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397787 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397788 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397787 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397789 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397787 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397790 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397787 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397791 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397787 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397792 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397787 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397787 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397787 1079 #edb/ref 17592186397788 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397787 1080 #uuid "440a36ad-3e7f-5834-9223-8af4fce0e717" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397788 1081 "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397788 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397788 1084 #uuid "9eee3b1f-7860-522c-8e25-2ab9122c7049" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397789 1081 "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{e}^{2x}y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397789 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397789 1084 #uuid "5eefff0b-47f1-50f6-aa20-45a25564434f" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397790 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{y}={x}^{3}" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397790 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397790 1084 #uuid "0dbcb751-5dd5-5c6c-8ef4-1f0333f1da29" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397791 1081 "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{y}" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397791 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397791 1084 #uuid "c6c9d281-4555-5a44-af3e-d9d7b6b8f65b" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397792 1081 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397792 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397792 1084 #uuid "54197164-398c-585a-83dd-5174235ce646" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397794 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397793 1069 "q-103991" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397793 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)}\\,\\text{d}x=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+C,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a+b.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397793 1072 #uuid "d8d217e5-8474-5d6b-93f2-6f6e1253c6f4" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397793 1074 "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)} & =\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x+6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n7=A(x+6)+B(x-1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(-6+6)+B(-6-1) \\\\ 7 & =-7B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(1+6)+B(1-1) \\\\ 7 & =7A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)} & =\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,b=-1,$ and $a+b=1-1=0.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397794 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397795 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397794 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397796 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397794 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397797 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397794 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397798 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397794 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397799 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397794 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397794 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397794 1079 #edb/ref 17592186397797 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397794 1080 #uuid "bdede32f-8b30-513b-878d-48ae1f1b992d" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397795 1081 "2" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397795 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397795 1084 #uuid "2ec330cc-8c77-5e4b-b2b9-ffed05aa242e" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397796 1081 "1" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397796 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397796 1084 #uuid "7c0961d6-77ee-5ef6-b56f-f97f7519ca2c" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397797 1081 "0" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397797 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397797 1084 #uuid "67f68e51-f432-57bf-9c04-5cee503b7449" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397798 1081 "-1" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186397798 1083 #edb/ref