gure out which of the cases we have in this situation, we find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$\n\nComputing $A-5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the 2nd  one). So, the rank of $A-5I$ is $1.$ Using the rank-nullity theorem, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-5I) & =3-\\text{rank}(A-5I) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nRecall that the nullity of $A-5I$ gives the number of eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$ Therefore, there must be two such eigenvectors, say, ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}.$ \nSo, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{3}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{2}(5)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(5)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398026 1075 #edb/ref 175