(relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-4t+8),{t}^{4}-\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398227 1072 #uuid "942f342e-d375-5134-864c-b37213f6cf64" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398227 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-4t+8),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-4}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{2}(t-2)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{2}(t-2)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=2, \\\\ 4{t}^{2}(t-2)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=2$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398228 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398229 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398228 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398230 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398228 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398231 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398228 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398232 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398228 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398233 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398228 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398228 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398228 1079 #edb/ref 17592186398232 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398228 1080 #uui