"The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,x>{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator of the exponent vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398243 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398244 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398245 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398246 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398242 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398247 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398242 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398242 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398242 1079 #edb/ref 17592186398246 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398242 1080 #uuid "48ae7e1a-6a16-5754-8819-ca18faede750" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398243 1081 "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x<{y}^{2}}" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398243 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 175921860455