ulate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+9t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t+4,6t+9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,3⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨3t+4,6t+9⟩ & =c⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨3t+4,6t+9⟩ & =⟨c,3c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3t+4=c \\\\ 6t+9=3c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=3t+4.$ Substituting $c=3t+4$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t+9 & =3(3t+4) \\\\ 6t+9 & =9t+12 \\\\ 3t & =-3 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨1,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(-1)^{2}+4(-1),3(-1)^{2}+9(-1)⟩=⟨-\\frac{5}{2},-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(-\\frac{5}{2},-6).$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398611 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398612 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398611 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398613 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398611 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398614 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398611 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398615 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398611 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398616 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398611 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398611 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398611 1079 #edb/ref 17592186398615 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398611 1080 #uuid "7805dc28-4db0-584d-a7f0-aae461b099c2" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398612 1081 "(14,30)" 13194139533472 175921860455