3{t}^{2}-10t,2t-4⟩,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-18,-8⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398693 1072 #uuid "d1d2200e-dbc1-5e87-951b-4fac16df86f2" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398693 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-10t,2t-4⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (3{t}^{2}-10t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (2t-4)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}-4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=⟨-18,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨(3)^{3}-5(3)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,(3)^{2}-4(3)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-18,-8⟩ & =⟨-18+{C}_{1},\\,-3+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}-4t-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}-4t-5⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}={t}^{2}(t-5) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0,5, \\\\ {t}^{2}-4t-5=(t+1)(t-5) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=5,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398694 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398695 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398694 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398696 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398694 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398697 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398694 1075