--- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398780 1072 #uuid "30b4ad39-4fd5-5a3d-94e7-8aa42628b59f" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398780 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈6.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2y-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 6-1 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 13-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ | $19$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=6+19=25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ | $19$ | $25$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{p}-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 25-1 \\\\  & =53.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ | $19$ | $25$ | $53$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 53+8\\cdot 13-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 368 \\\\  & =\\frac{92}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ | $19$ | $25$ | $53$ | $\\frac{92}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =6+\\frac{92}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ | $19$ | $25$ | $53$ | $\\frac{92}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{110}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{110}{3}.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398781 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398782 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398781 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398783 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398781 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398784 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398781 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398785 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398781 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398786 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398781 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398781 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398781 1079 #edb/ref 17592186398782 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398781 1080 #uuid "26144729-79ed-5f68-8080-4ebf67858955" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398782 1081 "\\frac{110}{3}" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398782 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398782 1084 #uuid "1e50564b-d0e1-5f01-bf1d-8aa9f12f3480" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398783 1081 "6" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398783 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398783 1084 #uuid "a408ca6f-702d-57b3-ab99-8e8d1d9925b6" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398784 1081 "25" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186398784 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 tr