186399110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399111 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399110 1069 "q-79494" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399110 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+81y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399110 1072 #uuid "dcd4b923-bea2-5168-a3b2-3077cdbbb018" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399110 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9R\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3=9R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399111 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399112 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399111 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399113 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399111 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399114 13194139533472 1759218