 the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1+1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1+1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399152 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399153 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399152 1075 #edb/ref 175921863