ixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-8t+3,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399172 1072 #uuid "355a01e3-70fb-59a2-890d-575b2107fb75" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399172 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-8t+3)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(1)^{3}-4(1)^{2}+3(1)+C \\\\ 0 & =1-4+3+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+3t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+3t=t(t-3)(t-1)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/9f/9f1dc2cc8c718f825c629bd92beb116326a36deb1dfbbe71e028cf1cf41445eb.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $1<t<3.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399174 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399175 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399176 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399177 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399178 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399173 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399173 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399173 1079 #ed