 17592186045585 true] [17592186399179 1072 #uuid "c60fbb60-b78a-5c24-90ef-165c91d4dd65" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399179 1074 "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.05S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the channel currently has $15\\,000$ subscribers, so we have ${S}_{0}=15\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of subscribers when $t=12,$ we substitute $t=12$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(12) & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05\\cdot 12} \\\\  & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.6} \\\\  & ≈27\\,331.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of subscribers is $27\\,332$ after $12$ weeks." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399181 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399182 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399183 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399184 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399185 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399180 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399180 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399180 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399182 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399180 1080 #uuid "714a5ee8-c0ff-5aa3-a5d9-82c81856f170" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399181 1081 "24\\,731" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399181 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399181 1084 #uuid "ad5e1f00-cc1f-5d4f-87c2-02b6b36f47ba" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399182 1081 "27\\,332" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399182 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399182 1084 #uuid "cec7db82-de58-50eb-a3aa-1156e21fbeca" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399183 1081 "22\\,377" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399183 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399183 1084 #uuid "34503797-dc86-5eff-9dba-4c2c0e4b5865" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399184 1081 "20\\,248" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399184 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399184 1084 #uuid "9c43e127-b476-5072-8782-64defe890eab" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399185 1081 "31\\,755" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399185 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399185 1084 #uuid "96af2a00-4233-59fc-a447-2f18d08406ac" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399187 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399186 1069 "q-83477" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399186 1071 "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x-y$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2).$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399186 1072 #uuid "97e3e680-a9e7-597f-a877-a0daf5245a87" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399186 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y-1,\\,x-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y-1=0 \\\\ x-1=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,1).$ Since the point $(1,1)$ is not in the interior of $D$,  we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the line $y=2-x,$ in the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)(0)-(t)-(0) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)(t)-(0)-(t) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨0,2⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t⟨-2,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2-2t,2t) \\\\  & =(2-2t)(2t)-(2-2t)-(2t) \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}+4t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (-4{t}^{2}+4t-2)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8t+4 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point $(1,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(2,0),\\,(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the point $(0,0).$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399188 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399189 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399190 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399191 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399192 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399187 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399187 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399187 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399191 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399187 1080 #uuid "249bd719-2909-5d27-a78e-0ce0dd0aaab3" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399188 1081 "3" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399188 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399188 1084 #uuid "37d0fe1a-19ca-5f80-a487-821d0d2f05da" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399189 1081 "2" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399189 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399189 1084 #uuid "6e287a52-db21-559f-9d89-5103b7019f80" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399190 1081 "1" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399190 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399190 1084 #uuid "f5969ae4-0089-56bd-bf20-0cf41f4333df" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399191 1081 "0" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399191 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399191 1084 #uuid "cd0d464b-7ff4-56b8-bcb7-57b51b72d32d" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399192 1081 "-1" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399192 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399192 1084 #uuid "db4a7149-d824-54c0-86ee-b21c01fb5cf4" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399194 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399193 1069 "q-83480" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399193 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399193 1072 #uuid "ae635709-cef0-5d90-bcf8-39aaac1bc474" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399193 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{9}{5}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399194 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399195 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399194 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399196 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399194 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399197 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399194 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399198 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399194 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399199 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399194 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399194 1077 "selection" 131941395