& 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399200 1072 #uuid "a11ee4d1-3e88-5212-80d0-293758418f1a" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399200 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-11{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+11{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{11}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{2} & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{7}{8}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399202 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399203 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399204 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399205 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399206 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399201 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399201 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399201 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399205 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399201 1080 #uuid "f7423f8b-a007-5757-8b22-bf2bd8ad4565" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399202 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399202 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399202 1084 #uuid "5cf764b3-b5dc-5b5a-abc6-619ed240e8d9" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399203 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399203 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399203 1084 #uuid "1ff8c962-ac33-5ade-9011-9b96b78cc434" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399204 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399204 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399204 1084 #uuid "3b5cfc50-7130-55a5-a100-906bbe37b827" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399205 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399205 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399205 1084 #uuid "946b0b50-f143-58dc-b90c-fba5b6dccff5" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399206 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399206 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399206 1084 #uuid "5869b5d8-1b29-59f0-8e8e-a890cf28490c" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399208 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399207 1069 "q-83505" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399207 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨8(1-2t)^{3},12(1+4t)^{2}⟩,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,-26⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399207 1072 #uuid "6c8cbd85-ace7-5114-80e4-3625b6d8dbe5" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399207 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8(1-2t)^{3},12(1+4t)^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8(1-2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 12(1+4t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-(1-2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,(1+4t)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨-1,-26⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨-(1-2(0))^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,(1+4(0))^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-26⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-27.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨-(1-2t)^{4},\\,(1+4t)^{3}-27⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-(1-2t)^{4},\\,(1+4t)^{3}-27⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(1-2t)^{4} & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ (1+4t)^{3}-27 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399209 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399210 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399211 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399212 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399213 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399208 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399208 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399208 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399212 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399208 1080 #uuid "7e571167-d51a-53d3-8b9f-8daca0904597" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399209 1081 "t=0" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399209 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399209 1084 #uuid "6e7db313-b4d5-5153-bcfa-dde6e87c4ffa" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399210 1081 "t=\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399210 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399210 1084 #uuid "97309839-825e-57b1-95c6-7824e0a74346" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399211 1081 "t=2" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399211 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399211 1084 #uuid "1cb2cae5-16fa-5704-83b1-6c31b690cd55" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399212 1081 "t=\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399212 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399212 1084 #uuid "e12f4381-2d6d-5cb7-86dc-4e456318069c" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399213 1081 "No stationary points" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399213 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399213 1084 #uuid "4958c111-ad18-51a1-bae1-88b3f960e07f" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399215 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399214 1069 "q-83826" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399214 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x+6?$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399214 1072 #uuid "6ee6c969-d319-57a0-b1a1-3435c9110a6c" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399214 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }7}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-7$ does not take the above form. The value $-7$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{7}{1},$ and the numerator $(7)$ is not a factor of $6.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399215 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399216 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399215 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399217 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [175921863