04" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399349 1071 "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum product of three positive numbers given that the sum of their squares is $108.$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399349 1072 #uuid "8e5f2d5d-b1b9-503b-9dbd-36de49acff81" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399349 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be three positive numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=108.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=108\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}yz=2\\lambda x \\\\ xz=2\\lambda y \\\\ xy=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}xyz=2\\lambda {x}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {y}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(6,6,6),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(6,6,6)=6\\cdot 6\\cdot 6=216.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,2,2) & =10\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & <f(6,6,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399350 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399351 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399350 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399352 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399350 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399353 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399350 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399354 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399350 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399355 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399350 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399350 1077 "selection" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399350 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399353 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399350 1080 #uuid "25347463-2d58-522d-8979-b8544d1335f3" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399351 1081 "105\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399351 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399351 1084 #uuid "08059ff9-d83f-540a-a482-2a8f806a18c0" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399352 1081 "125" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399352 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399352 1084 #uuid "40d83586-f330-5ddd-a884-c230dd198024" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399353 1081 "216" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399353 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399353 1084 #uuid "591f3fa0-6797-5340-8094-49d07c9c3d89" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399354 1081 "256" 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399354 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533472 17592186045585 true] [17592186399354 1084 #uuid "44d35100-e5d6-5e35-ac5b-f307d1753f6e" 13194139